'''2.''' A utensil, ornament, or other article made of brass: as, to clean the ''brasses'' on board a
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Brass of Eleanor Bohun (died 1399), in Westminster Abbey.}}}}
ship.—'''3.''' In ''mach.'', a pillow, bearing, collar, box, or bush, supporting a gudgeon: so called because frequently made of brass.—'''4.''' In ''medieval archæol.'', a funeral monument consisting of a plate of brass, usually of rectangular shape and often of large size, incised with an effigy, coats of arms, inscriptions, and frequently accessory ornament. {{smaller|Such brasses are sometimes splendidly enameled. In some examples the designs are executed in relief, or in relief in combination with engraving. Slabs of stone inlaid with figures, etc., in brass are also called brasses, and are a usual form of medieval monument. Both the plates of brass and the inlaid stones were frequently placed in the ordinary pavement of churches. Comparatively few of such monuments executed wholly in brass survive, as the value of the metal has caused it to be melted down and applied to other uses.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Among the knightly ''brasses'' of the graves,
And by the cold Hic Jacets of the dead.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Merlin and Vivien.}}}}
'''5.''' A brass musical instrument, or, collectively, the brass instruments in a band or an orchestra.—'''6.''' Money. [Now only colloq.]

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>Withouten pite, pilour! pore men thou robbedest,
And beere heor ''bras'' on thi bac to Caleys to sulle.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), iii. 189.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>We should scorn each bribing varlet’s ''brass.''</poem>
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Satires, IV. v. 12.}}}}

{{smaller block|Trying to get out of debt, a very ancient slough, called by the Latins aes alienum, another’s ''brass'', for some of their coins were made of brass; still living, and dying, and buried by this other’s ''brass.''
{{right|''Thoreau'', Walden, p. 9.}}}}

'''7.''' In ''coal-mining'', iron pyrites. {{smaller|It occurs in small particles disseminated through the coal, or in veinlets or thin scaly partings. [Rarely used except in the plural.]}} —'''8.''' Excessive assurance; impudence; brazenness: as, he has ''brass'' enough for anything. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|She in her defence made him appear such a rogue that the chief justice wondered he had the ''brass'' to appear in a court of justice.
{{right|''Roger North'', Examen, p. 256.}}}}

{{smaller block|To me he appears the most impudent piece of ''brass'' that ever spoke with a tongue.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', She Stoops to Conquer, iii.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Brass-blacking.''' See ''blacking.''—'''Brass-color,''' in ''glass-making'', a preparation for staining glass, made by exposing thin brass plates upon tiles in the annealing-arch of a glass-house until they are completely oxidized into a black powder. This powder, fused with glass, gives various tints of green and turquoise.—'''Brass-foil,''' or '''brass-leaf,''' Dutch leaf or Dutch gold, formed by beating out plates of brass to extreme thinness.—'''Brass-powder,''' copper and its various alloys ground to fine powder and used with varnish for decorative purposes. Many of the so-called bronze-powders are brass-powders.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Made or composed of brass; pertaining to or resembling brass; brazen; brassy.

{{smaller block|<poem>Trumpet, blow loud,
Send thy ''brass'' voice through all these lazy tents.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., i. 3.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Brass instrument.''' See ''wind-instrument.''—'''Brass rule.''' See ''rule.''}}

